{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And He said, Jacob, Jacob.</b> After HaShem said to him, \"Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel shall be your name\" (Genesis 35:10), it would have been fitting that He call him by that honored name. And so it is mentioned in this pericope three times. But He called him Jacob to hint that now he would no longer strive with Elohim and with men and prevail, but rather he would be in the house of servants until He bring him up on high as well, for from now the exile would begin with him. And this is the reason for \"And these are the names of the children of Israel who were coming into Egypt: Jacob and his sons\" (Genesis 46:8) — for by the name children of Israel they would come there, for the sons would be fruitful and multiply and their name and honor would grow great, but Jacob is he himself now in his descent there. And Scripture mentioned Er and Onan along with the names of the children of Israel who were coming into Egypt for a known sod from our words that we have already written (Ramban on Genesis 38:8), and the enlightened will understand this and the reason for the entire verse. And so Scripture mentioned them in the census (Numbers 26:19–20), \"The sons of Judah: Er and Onan; and Er and Onan died in the land of Canaan; and the sons of Judah were according to their families,\" and in the words of Chronicles (1 Chronicles 2:3–4) it counted them by number, saying, \"The sons of Judah: Er and Onan and Shelah — three were born to him from the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess; and Tamar his daughter-in-law bore him Perez and Zerah. All the sons of Judah: five.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:31.517614716Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}